description:
LOCATION: crate-jmap-contacts-server/src/lib.rs:59-60

    /// Capability URI for `urn:ietf:params:jmap:contacts`.
    pub use jmap_contacts_types::JMAP_CONTACTS_URI;

PROBLEM:
The doc string for a public constant intended for inclusion in JMAP
session capability advertisements has one sentence. A reader who
encounters this constant — likely while wiring up their HTTP transport
and trying to figure out which capabilities to advertise — gets no
guidance on:

1. **Where to put it.** Goes in the Session object's `capabilities`
   map (RFC 8620 §2.1). The README mentions this at line 199 but the
   constant itself doesn't link.

2. **What it advertises.** RFC 9610 defines exactly one capability
   URI for the contacts extension, covering AddressBook and
   ContactCard. A reader should not have to fetch RFC 9610 to learn
   what enabling this capability means.

3. **What other URIs combine with it.** Most consumers will also
   want `urn:ietf:params:jmap:core` (RFC 8620 §2). The doc doesn't
   point at where those constants live.

4. **Versioning.** Is this URI stable across RFC revisions? Yes, but
   the doc doesn't say so, and a careful reader would have to RFC-
   hunt.

The README §"Capability URI" at lines 192-200 has some of this
content but it's not visible from rustdoc.

WHAT CALLERS CANNOT DEPEND ON:
That `cargo doc` output for this constant tells them what to do with
it.

RECOMMENDED:
Expand the doc to a paragraph:

    /// Capability URI for the JMAP for Contacts extension (RFC 9610).
    ///
    /// Include this string in your Session object's `capabilities` map
    /// (RFC 8620 §2.1) to advertise support for AddressBook and
    /// ContactCard objects to clients. Typically combined with
    /// `urn:ietf:params:jmap:core` from `jmap-server`.
    ///
    /// Example:
    /// ```
    /// # use jmap_contacts_server::JMAP_CONTACTS_URI;
    /// assert_eq!(JMAP_CONTACTS_URI, "urn:ietf:params:jmap:contacts");
    /// ```
    pub use jmap_contacts_types::JMAP_CONTACTS_URI;
    // (or local pub const — see JMAP-qz9v.32)

P3 because the constant works as-is; the documentation gap is cost-
of-discovery, not a runtime concern. Pairs with JMAP-qz9v.32 (the
re-export-vs-local-const finding).
